            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.222,
            0.293,
            0.242
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Figure it Out"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.246,
            0.726,
            0.267
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Represent the following numbers in the Roman system."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.22,
            0.275,
            0.707,
            0.295
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) 1222 (ii) 2999 (iii) 302 (iv) 715"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.301,
            0.849,
            0.396
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We see how vastly efficient this system is compared to some of the previous number systems that we have seen. This system seems to have evolved out of the ancient Greek number system in around the 8th century BCE in Rome, and evolved over time. It spread throughout Europe with the expansion of the Roman empire."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.421,
            0.834,
            0.517
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The efficiency of this system is due to the grouping of a given number by not just one group size, but a sequence of group sizes that we call landmark numbers, and then using these landmark numbers to represent the given number. This idea is the next important breakthrough in the history of the evolution of number systems."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.539,
            0.848,
            0.598
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Despite the relative efficiency of the Roman system, it doesn’t lend itself to an easy performance of arithmetic operations, particularly multiplication and division."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.607,
            0.846,
            0.645
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "？ "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Try adding the following numbers without converting them to Hindu numerals:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.207,
            0.655,
            0.449,
            0.674
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(a) CCXXXII + CCCCXIII"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.674,
            0.771,
            0.712
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us find the total number of Is, Xs, and Cs, and group them starting from the largest landmark number."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.711,
            0.772,
            0.749
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Apparently, it looks like the largest landmark number is C, but note that 5 Cs (100s) make a D (500). So the sum is"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.789,
            0.659,
            0.896,
            0.782
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 15,
              "bbox": [
                0.789,
                0.659,
                0.896,
                0.782
              ],
